.\" -*- mode: troff; coding: utf-8 -*-
.TH GENFSTAB 8
.SH NAME
genfstab \- generate an fstab(5) from a mounted system
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
\fBgenfstab\fR inspects the currently mounted filesystems and prints
fstab(5) lines for the mounts found under the given root. It is
typically used when installing or rescuing a system: chroot or mount the
target, then capture its mounts into \fI/etc/fstab\fR.
.LP
Block device mounts are printed with \fBUUID=\fR (default) or
\fBLABEL=\fR identifiers resolved through \fBblkid\fR(8); bind mounts
are printed with \fInone\fR as the device and the \fBbind\fR option;
pseudo filesystems (proc, sysfs, devpts, tmpfs on \fI/run\fR and
\fI/dev/shm\fR) are listed without device identifiers. Mounts outside
the given root are ignored.
.LP
The result is printed to standard output; redirect it to \fI/etc/fstab\fR
on the target.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-U\fR
Use UUID identifiers for block devices (default).
.TP
\fB\-L\fR
Use filesystem labels for block devices.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR
Also print pseudo filesystems (proc, sysfs, devpts, tmpfs) explicitly.
By default only entries that belong in a persistent fstab are printed.
.TP
\fI<root>\fR
Root of the mounted system (default \fI/\fR).

.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
Generate fstab for a system mounted at /mnt:
.RS
\f(CRgenfstab /mnt > /mnt/etc/fstab\fR
.RE
.LP
Regenerate fstab for the running system using labels:
.RS
\f(CRgenfstab \-L / >> /etc/fstab\fR
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fstab (5),
.BR mount (8),
.BR blkid (8),
.BR qlchroot (8)

